== format ==
key = {
	type = scope


	text = {
		trigger = { ... }
		localization_key = string
		fallback = yes # Optional; will cause this one to be picked if no 
	}

	...

	random_valid = yes # Optional, will randomize instead of picking first valid
}

You can also add variants:
key = {
	parent = some_custom_loc_key
	suffix = "_suffix"
	fallback = true # Continue falling back if the parent+suffix doesnt have a loc key
}
The logic of the parent will be run, then the suffix is added to the custom loc key.

When you want to use a custom loc in the localization then use: [<scope>.Custom('<key>')]

== Steam Rich Presence status (rich_presence_status.txt) ==
GetRichPresenceStatus resolves the status line shown in the Steam friends list.
It is evaluated for the player's country (ROOT = country); the first text block whose trigger
passes wins, and the final fallback = yes block is the default.

To add a new status: add a text block with a trigger and a localization_key (defined in
main_menu/localization/english/rich_presence_l_english.yml) ABOVE the fallback entry. No C++
change is needed. Two authoring rules:
- The country name is composed by the Steam-side display template ("%status% as %country%, ..."),
  so do not include the player's country in a status string, and never end a status with "as".
- Loc strings must only use *WithNoTooltip name functions: tooltip-wrapped names (GetName) embed
  markup that Steam renders as raw text.
